00:43:49.470 --> 00:43:52.122
how smart they are, so it's subtly vicious.
展示自己多么聪明，暗中较劲。

866
00:43:52.122 --> 00:43:53.580
So whenever you have an opportunity
所以每当有机会

867
00:43:53.580 --> 00:43:55.140
to have an examining committee that's
让评审委员会里

868
00:43:55.140 --> 00:43:59.490
full of people with gray hair, that's what you want.
全是满头银发的长者，那正是你想要的。

869
00:43:59.490 --> 00:44:01.500
Well, that's just a word or two about something
好吧，关于这点我只说这一两句，

870
00:44:01.500 --> 00:44:02.460
I haven't listed here.
这点我还没列在这里。

871
00:44:02.460 --> 00:44:05.610
Let's get into the subject of job talks.
现在我们进入求职演讲的话题。

872
00:44:05.610 --> 00:44:14.230
So I was sitting in a bar many years ago in San Diego.
很多年前，我坐在圣地亚哥的一家酒吧里。

873
00:44:14.230 --> 00:44:16.680
I was a member of the Navy Science Board,
我是海军科学委员会的成员，

874
00:44:16.680 --> 00:44:21.420
and I was sitting with a couple of my colleagues on the board
当时和委员会的两位同事坐在一起。

875
00:44:21.420 --> 00:44:24.910
Delores Etter from the University of Colorado.
一位是科罗拉多大学的 Delores Etter。

876
00:44:24.910 --> 00:44:27.960
She made me so jealous I could spit because she'd written 21
她让我嫉妒得牙痒，因为她已写了 21 本书，

877
00:44:27.960 --> 00:44:30.660
books, and I'd only written 17.
而我只写了 17 本。

878
00:44:30.660 --> 00:44:35.130
And then the other one was Bill Weldon
另一位是 Bill Weldon，

879
00:44:35.130 --> 00:44:38.230
from the University of Texas.
来自德克萨斯大学。

880
00:44:38.230 --> 00:44:41.280
He was an electromagnetism guy, and he
他专攻电磁学，

881
00:44:41.280 --> 00:44:45.030
knew how to use rail guns to drive steel rods through tank
懂得如何用轨道炮把钢棒

882
00:44:45.030 --> 00:44:45.660
armor.
射穿坦克装甲。

883
00:44:45.660 --> 00:44:47.590
These were interesting people.
这些人都很有意思。

884
00:44:47.590 --> 00:44:53.880
So I said, what do you look for in a faculty candidate?
我便问，你们看重求职者的什么？

885
00:44:53.880 --> 00:44:58.000
And within one microsecond, Delores
话音刚落，Delores

886
00:44:58.000 --> 00:45:00.270
said, they have to show us they've
立刻答道，他们必须让我们看到

887
00:45:00.270 --> 00:45:05.940
got some kind of vision, quickly followed by Bill who said,
他们具有某种远见。Bill 紧跟着说：

888
00:45:05.940 --> 00:45:08.063
they have to show us that they've done something.
他们必须让我们看到他们做出了成果。

889
00:45:08.063 --> 00:45:15.160

890
00:45:15.160 --> 00:45:18.550
Oh, that sounds good, I said.
「嗯，说得好。」我说。

891
00:45:18.550 --> 00:45:20.560
And then I said to them, how long
然后我问他们，

892
00:45:20.560 --> 00:45:24.640
does a candidate have to establish these two things?
求职者需要多久建立这两点？

893
00:45:24.640 --> 00:45:26.850
What do you think?
你觉得呢？

894
00:45:26.850 --> 00:45:28.510
Well, compare your answer to theirs.
好，把你的答案跟他们比一比。

895
00:45:28.510 --> 00:45:33.970

896
00:45:33.970 --> 00:45:36.803
Five minutes.
五分钟。

897
00:45:36.803 --> 00:45:38.470
So if you haven't expressed your vision,
所以如果你还没表达你的愿景，

898
00:45:38.470 --> 00:45:42.850
if you haven't told people that you've done something in five
还没在五分钟内告诉别人你做了什么，

899
00:45:42.850 --> 00:45:45.880
minutes, you've already lost.
那你就输了。

900
00:45:45.880 --> 00:45:47.630
So you have to be able to do that.
所以你必须能完成这件事。

901
00:45:47.630 --> 00:45:51.250
And let me just mention a couple of things in that connection.
关于这一点，我想再提几件事。

902
00:45:51.250 --> 00:45:54.190
Here, the vision is in part, a problem
在这里，愿景一部分是某个问题——

903
00:45:54.190 --> 00:46:00.340
that somebody cares about and something new in your approach.
有人关心的问题，以及你方法中的新意。

904
00:46:00.340 --> 00:46:06.350

905
00:46:06.350 --> 00:46:08.560
So the problem is understanding the nature
所以这个问题是理解

906
00:46:08.560 --> 00:46:10.480
of human intelligence.
人类智能的本质。

907
00:46:10.480 --> 00:46:12.640
And the approach is asking questions
而方法是提出这样的问题：

908
00:46:12.640 --> 00:46:16.510
about what makes us different from chimpanzees
是什么让我们与黑猩猩

909
00:46:16.510 --> 00:46:18.130
and Neanderthals.
和尼安德特人不同。

910
00:46:18.130 --> 00:46:19.720
Is it merely a matter of quantity,
这只是数量上的差别吗，

911
00:46:19.720 --> 00:46:23.320
or we're just a little bit smarter in some continuous way?
还是我们在某个连续维度上聪明了一点点？

912
00:46:23.320 --> 00:46:26.490
Or do we have something that's fundamentally different
或者我们拥有某种本质不同的东西——

913
00:46:26.490 --> 00:46:30.550
that chimpanzees don't have and Neanderthals either?
黑猩猩没有，尼安德特人也没有？

914
00:46:30.550 --> 00:46:34.630
And the answer is yes, we do have something different.
答案是肯定的，我们确实拥有不同的东西。

915
00:46:34.630 --> 00:46:36.900
We are symbolic creatures.
我们是使用符号的生物。

916
00:46:36.900 --> 00:46:39.550
And because we're symbolic creatures,
因为我们是使用符号的生物，

917
00:46:39.550 --> 00:46:44.560
we can build symbolic descriptions
我们能够构建符号化的描述，

918
00:46:44.560 --> 00:46:46.330
of relations and events.
来描述关系与事件。

919
00:46:46.330 --> 00:46:48.835
We can string them together and make stories.
我们能把它们串联起来，构成故事。

920
00:46:48.835 --> 00:46:51.760
And because we can make stories, that's what makes us different.
也因为我们会讲故事，这就是我们的不同之处。

921
00:46:51.760 --> 00:46:54.190
So that's my stump speech.
这就是我的开场套话。

922
00:46:54.190 --> 00:46:56.560
That's how I start most of my talks
这是我在大多数关于

923
00:46:56.560 --> 00:46:59.260
on my own personal research.
我个人研究的演讲中的开场。

924
00:46:59.260 --> 00:47:04.990
How do you express the notion that you've done something?
你如何表达你确实做出了成果？

925
00:47:04.990 --> 00:47:10.240
By listing the steps that need to be taken in order to achieve
通过列出为实现这一目标

926
00:47:10.240 --> 00:47:12.602
the solution to that problem.
需要采取的步骤。

927
00:47:12.602 --> 00:47:14.560
You don't have to have done all of those steps.
你不必已经完成所有这些步骤。

928
00:47:14.560 --> 00:47:18.370
But you can say here's what needs to be done.
但你可以说：这是需要做的事。

929
00:47:18.370 --> 00:47:20.660
An example, here's what needs to be done.
举个例子，这些事需要做。

930
00:47:20.660 --> 00:47:23.260
We need to specify some behavior.
我们需要规定某些行为。

931
00:47:23.260 --> 00:47:25.840
We need to enumerate the constraints that
我们需要列举那些

932
00:47:25.840 --> 00:47:28.690
make it possible to deal with that behavior.
让处理这些行为成为可能的约束。

933
00:47:28.690 --> 00:47:31.030
We have to implement a system because we're engineers,
我们必须实现一个系统，因为我们是工程师，

934
00:47:31.030 --> 00:47:33.190
and we don't think that we've understood something
我们不认为我们理解了某件事，

935
00:47:33.190 --> 00:47:35.333
unless we can build it.
除非我们能把它造出来。

936
00:47:35.333 --> 00:47:37.000
And we've built such a system, and we're
我们已经造出了这样的一个系统，

937
00:47:37.000 --> 00:47:38.542
about to demonstrate it to you today.
今天稍后就会演示给你看。

938
00:47:38.542 --> 00:47:42.040
That would be an example of enumerating a series of steps
这便是列举实现愿景

939
00:47:42.040 --> 00:47:45.290
needed to realize the vision.
所需步骤的一个范例。

940
00:47:45.290 --> 00:47:49.280
So then blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.
然后呢，吧啦吧啦吧啦吧啦吧啦。

941
00:47:49.280 --> 00:47:51.250
And then you conclude by--
然后你以——

942
00:47:51.250 --> 00:47:54.550
you conclude by enumerating your contributions.
以列举你的贡献作为收尾。

943
00:47:54.550 --> 00:48:01.110

944
00:48:01.110 --> 00:48:03.930
It's kind of mirror of these steps.
它与前面的步骤互为镜像。

945
00:48:03.930 --> 00:48:06.610
And it helps to establish that you've done something.
这有助于确立你确实做出了成果。

946
00:48:06.610 --> 00:48:08.730
So that's a kind of general purpose framework
这就是一个通用框架，

947
00:48:08.730 --> 00:48:12.180
for doing a technical talk.
用来做一场技术演讲。

948
00:48:12.180 --> 00:48:16.930
Now, only a few more things left to do today.
今天剩下的内容只剩几件事。

949
00:48:16.930 --> 00:48:19.360
Getting famous is the next item on our agenda
议程上的下一项是——出名。

950
00:48:19.360 --> 00:48:20.920
because once you've got the job, you
因为一旦你拿到职位，

951
00:48:20.920 --> 00:48:22.420
need to think a little bit about how
你就得稍微想想

952
00:48:22.420 --> 00:48:25.790
you're going to be recognized for what you do.
如何让你的工作被人认可。

953
00:48:25.790 --> 00:48:38.140
So oh, first of all, why should you care about getting famous?
那么，先问一句，你为什么要在意出名？

954
00:48:38.140 --> 00:48:40.360
I thought about this in connection
我想起一件与此事有关的事，

955
00:48:40.360 --> 00:48:44.920
with a fundraising event I attended
那是我参加的一场募捐活动，

956
00:48:44.920 --> 00:48:49.920
once, a fundraising event for raising money
一场为募集善款而办的募捐会——

957
00:48:49.920 --> 00:48:52.680
to save Venice from going under water and having all of its art
为了拯救威尼斯免于沉入水下，

958
00:48:52.680 --> 00:48:53.820
destroyed.
让它所有的艺术品毁于一旦。

959
00:48:53.820 --> 00:49:00.210
Anyway, I was sitting here, and JC was sitting here.
总之，我坐在这里，茱莉亚坐在那里。

960
00:49:00.210 --> 00:49:03.210
That was Julia, the late Julia Child.
那就是茱莉亚，已故的茱莉亚·查尔德。

961
00:49:03.210 --> 00:49:08.190

962
00:49:08.190 --> 00:49:10.950
And as the evening wore on, more and more people
夜渐深，越来越多的人

963
00:49:10.950 --> 00:49:14.760
would come up and ask Julia to autograph something
走上前请茱莉亚签名，

964
00:49:14.760 --> 00:49:19.590
or express a feeling that she had changed their life.
或者表达她改变了他们人生的感触。

965
00:49:19.590 --> 00:49:23.380
And it just happened over and over again.
这样的情形反复出现。

966
00:49:23.380 --> 00:49:27.810
So eventually, I turned to Julia, and I said, Ms. Child,
于是我终于转向茱莉亚，问：查尔德女士，

967
00:49:27.810 --> 00:49:30.157
is it fun to be famous?
出名有意思吗？

968
00:49:30.157 --> 00:49:31.740
And she thought about it for a second.
她想了想。

969
00:49:31.740 --> 00:49:34.752
And she said, you get used to it.
她说，你会习惯的。

970
00:49:34.752 --> 00:49:35.900
[LAUGHTER]
【笑声】

971
00:49:35.900 --> 00:49:38.070
But you know what occured to me?
但你知道我后来想到了什么？

972
00:49:38.070 --> 00:49:41.590
You never get used to being ignored.
你永远无法习惯被忽视。

973
00:49:41.590 --> 00:49:45.240
So it's-- here's a way to think about it.
所以——可以这样想。

974
00:49:45.240 --> 00:49:47.100
Your ideas are like your children.
你的想法就像你的孩子。

975
00:49:47.100 --> 00:49:50.370
And you don't want them to go into the world in rags.
你不会愿意让他们衣衫褴褛地走入世界。

976
00:49:50.370 --> 00:49:51.870
So what you want to do is to be sure
所以你想要做的是确保

977
00:49:51.870 --> 00:49:55.390
that you have these techniques, these mechanisms,
你掌握这些技巧、这些方法，

978
00:49:55.390 --> 00:49:58.470
these thoughts about how to present ideas that you have so
知道如何呈现你的想法，

979
00:49:58.470 --> 00:50:02.280
that they're recognized for the value that is in them.
好让它们因其中的价值而被认可。

980
00:50:02.280 --> 00:50:05.370
So that's why it's a legitimate thing to concern yourself
这就是为什么讲究包装

981
00:50:05.370 --> 00:50:08.010
with packaging.
是一件理所应当的事。

982
00:50:08.010 --> 00:50:11.070
Now, how do you get remembered?
那么，怎样才能让人记住你？

983
00:50:11.070 --> 00:50:14.720
Well, there's something I like to call Winston's star.
我有个喜欢称之为「温斯顿之星」的东西。

984
00:50:14.720 --> 00:50:20.130
And every one of the items I'm about to articulate starts
接下来我要讲的每一项

985
00:50:20.130 --> 00:50:24.510
with an S. So if you want your presentation ideas
都以 S 开头。所以，如果你想让你的演示

986
00:50:24.510 --> 00:50:27.240
to be remembered, one of the things you need to do
中的想法被人记住，你需要做的

987
00:50:27.240 --> 00:50:32.820
is to make sure that you have some kind of symbol associated
一件事是：确保你的工作配有一个

988
00:50:32.820 --> 00:50:34.590
with your work.
对应的标志。

989
00:50:34.590 --> 00:50:37.980
So this arch example is actually from my PhD thesis
这个拱门的例子其实来自我的博士论文，

990
00:50:37.980 --> 00:50:39.780
many, many years ago.
那是很多很多年前的事了。

991
00:50:39.780 --> 00:50:45.000
And in the course of my work at that time,
在我当时的研究过程中，

992
00:50:45.000 --> 00:50:47.820
this work on arch learning became mildly famous,
这项关于拱门学习的工作小有名气，

993
00:50:47.820 --> 00:50:49.770
and I didn't know why.
我却不知道为什么。

994
00:50:49.770 --> 00:50:51.630
It was only many years later that I realized
直到多年后我才意识到，

995
00:50:51.630 --> 00:50:54.660
that that work accidentally had all of the elements
那项工作碰巧具备了所有要素。

996
00:50:54.660 --> 00:50:56.220
on this star.
在这颗星上。

997
00:50:56.220 --> 00:50:59.910
So the first element is that there was a kind of symbol.
所以第一个元素就是：它有某种符号。

998
00:50:59.910 --> 00:51:00.900
It's the arch itself.
那就是拱门本身。

999
00:51:00.900 --> 00:51:05.510

1000
00:51:05.510 --> 00:51:10.480
Next thing you need is some kind of slogan,
接下来你需要的是某种口号，

1001
00:51:10.480 --> 00:51:13.740
a kind of phrase that provides a handle on the work.
一个能让人把握住这项工作的短语。

1002
00:51:13.740 --> 00:51:17.160
And in this case, the phrase was one shot learning.
在这里，那个短语就是 one shot learning。

1003
00:51:17.160 --> 00:51:20.120

1004
00:51:20.120 --> 00:51:23.340
And it was one shot because the program I wrote
之所以是“one shot”，是因为我写的程序

1005
00:51:23.340 --> 00:51:27.030
learned something definite from every example that
从每一个呈现给它的例子里

1006
00:51:27.030 --> 00:51:28.860
was presented to us.
都学到了某些确定的东西。

1007
00:51:28.860 --> 00:51:32.970
So in going from a model based on this configuration
所以当从一个基于这个构型的模型

1008
00:51:32.970 --> 00:51:35.940
to something that isn't an arch base on that configuration,
转向一个不是那种构型拱门的东西时，

1009
00:51:35.940 --> 00:51:38.640
the program learned that it has to be on top,
程序学到的是：它必须在顶上，

1010
00:51:38.640 --> 00:51:41.490
one shot learning.
这就是“one shot learning”。

1011
00:51:41.490 --> 00:51:45.210
So that's a symbol, slogan.
所以这就是符号和口号。

1012
00:51:45.210 --> 00:51:46.575
And now we need a surprise.
现在我们需要一个惊喜。

1013
00:51:46.575 --> 00:51:51.330

1014
00:51:51.330 --> 00:51:54.290
Yeah, the surprise is you don't need a million examples
没错，惊喜就在于：你不需要一百万个例子

1015
00:51:54.290 --> 00:51:55.910
of something to learn.
才能学会某件事。

1016
00:51:55.910 --> 00:51:58.010
You can do it with one example if you're
只要一个例子就够了——如果你足够聪明，

1017
00:51:58.010 --> 00:52:02.180
smart enough to make use of that example appropriately.
能够恰当地利用那个例子的话。

1018
00:52:02.180 --> 00:52:03.230
So that was the surprise.
这就是那个惊喜。

1019
00:52:03.230 --> 00:52:07.160
You can learn something definite from each example.
你可以从每一个例子里学到确定的东西。

1020
00:52:07.160 --> 00:52:08.840
Next item was a salient idea.
接下来是一项“突出的想法”。

1021
00:52:08.840 --> 00:52:13.280

1022
00:52:13.280 --> 00:52:16.785
Now, when I say salient idea, I don't mean important.
现在，我所说的“突出的想法”，不是指重要。

1023
00:52:16.785 --> 00:52:18.410
What I mean is an idea that sticks out.
我指的是一个能“跳出来”的想法。

1024
00:52:18.410 --> 00:52:21.260

1025
00:52:21.260 --> 00:52:24.080
Some theses, funnily enough, have too many good ideas,
有趣的是，有些论文反而有太多好点子，

1026
00:52:24.080 --> 00:52:27.110
and you don't know what it's all about because which one is it?
你反而搞不清楚它到底是讲什么的——到底哪一个？

1027
00:52:27.110 --> 00:52:29.290
So you need an idea that sticks out.
所以你需要一个能跳出来的想法。

1028
00:52:29.290 --> 00:52:33.890
And the idea that stuck out here was the notion of a near miss.
而这里跳出来的想法，就是“near miss”这个概念。

1029
00:52:33.890 --> 00:52:39.280

1030
00:52:39.280 --> 00:52:43.650
You see, this is not an arch, but it doesn't miss by much.
你看，这虽然不是拱门，但也差得不多。

1031
00:52:43.650 --> 00:52:45.490
So it's a near miss.
所以这就是“near miss”。

1032
00:52:45.490 --> 00:52:50.920
And finally, you need to tell the story
最后，你需要把故事讲出来——

1033
00:52:50.920 --> 00:52:56.160
of how you did it, how it works, why it's important.
你是怎么做的，它为什么能 work，为什么重要。

1034
00:52:56.160 --> 00:53:01.720
So that's a bit on how to not so much get famous,
所以这就是一点关于“怎样不那么出名”，

1035
00:53:01.720 --> 00:53:06.240
but how to ensure that your work is recognized.
而是怎样确保你的工作得到认可。

1036
00:53:06.240 --> 00:53:08.890
Well, we're almost finished because now we're
好，我们差不多讲完了，因为现在我们

1037
00:53:08.890 --> 00:53:13.470
down to this last item, which is how to stop.
来到了最后一项：怎么停。

1038
00:53:13.470 --> 00:53:14.998
And when we come to that, there's
讲到那里时，会涉及一个问题：

1039
00:53:14.998 --> 00:53:17.290
a question of all right, well, what is the final slide?
那好，最后一张幻灯片放什么？

1040
00:53:17.290 --> 00:53:22.412

1041
00:53:22.412 --> 00:53:23.620
And what are the final words?
最后的结束语又要说什么？

1042
00:53:23.620 --> 00:53:30.610

1043
00:53:30.610 --> 00:53:32.830
So for the final slide, let me give you
所以关于最后一张幻灯片，让我给你们看几个

1044
00:53:32.830 --> 00:53:34.750
some examples of possibilities.
可能的例子。

1045
00:53:34.750 --> 00:53:37.588

1046
00:53:37.588 --> 00:53:38.380
How about this one?
这张怎么样？

1047
00:53:38.380 --> 00:53:42.150

1048
00:53:42.150 --> 00:53:50.610
Well, you might see that slide and think to yourself,
你看到这张幻灯片，可能心里会想：

1049
00:53:50.610 --> 00:53:53.730
there are 1,000 faculty at MIT.
MIT 有一千位教职员工。

1050
00:53:53.730 --> 00:53:55.770
Nice piece of work, but not so much,
这工作不错，但也就那样——

1051
00:53:55.770 --> 00:54:01.050
but it's only a tiny piece of work if you divide by 1,000.
被一千一分，也就只是非常小的一小份了。

1052
00:54:01.050 --> 00:54:03.810
So when you show a whole gigantic list of collaborators
所以当你在演讲末尾列出一长串合作者时，

1053
00:54:03.810 --> 00:54:07.380
at the end of a talk, it's a kind of let down
这其实有点让人泄气——

1054
00:54:07.380 --> 00:54:09.150
because it suggests that nobody knows.
因为它暗示着：没人知道你真正做了什么。

1055
00:54:09.150 --> 00:54:12.650
Well, did you do anything significant?
你到底做没做出什么重要的东西？

1056
00:54:12.650 --> 00:54:15.180
Now, you've got to recognize your collaborators, right?
当然，你得感谢你的合作者，对吧？

1057
00:54:15.180 --> 00:54:16.970
So where do you do that?
那要放在哪儿？

1058
00:54:16.970 --> 00:54:19.877
Not on the last slide, on the first slide.
不是放在最后一张，而是放在第一张。

1059
00:54:19.877 --> 00:54:21.210
All this was on the first slide.
所有这些信息都在第一张上。

1060
00:54:21.210 --> 00:54:23.263
These are the collaborators, so you don't
这些人就是合作者，所以你不该

1061
00:54:23.263 --> 00:54:24.430
want to put them at the end.
把他们放在最后。

1062
00:54:24.430 --> 00:54:25.805
You don't want a slide like this.
你不想要这样的幻灯片。

1063
00:54:25.805 --> 00:54:28.290

1064
00:54:28.290 --> 00:54:31.060
How about this one?
那这张呢？

1065
00:54:31.060 --> 00:54:33.050
This is the worst possible way to end a talk.
这是结束一场演讲最糟糕的方式。

1066
00:54:33.050 --> 00:54:34.700
[LAUGHTER]
[笑声]

1067
00:54:34.700 --> 00:54:39.410
Because this slide can be up there for 20 minutes.
因为这张幻灯片可以挂在上面长达 20 分钟。

1068
00:54:39.410 --> 00:54:40.660
I've seen it happen.
我亲眼见过。

1069
00:54:40.660 --> 00:54:43.820
It squanders real estate.
这是在浪费版面。

1070
00:54:43.820 --> 00:54:47.420
It squanders an opportunity to tell people who you are.
它浪费了一次让人知道你“是谁”的机会。

1071
00:54:47.420 --> 00:54:48.200
It's just--
它只是——

1072
00:54:48.200 --> 00:54:51.777

1073
00:54:51.777 --> 00:54:52.610
What about this one?
那这张呢？

1074
00:54:52.610 --> 00:54:56.780

1075
00:54:56.780 --> 00:54:57.800
I often see it.
我经常看到这种。

1076
00:54:57.800 --> 00:55:01.300
I never see anybody write it down.
但从来没见过有人把它记下来。

1077
00:55:01.300 --> 00:55:03.991
Also, it wastes opportunity.
而且，它也在浪费机会。

1078
00:55:03.991 --> 00:55:06.580

1079
00:55:06.580 --> 00:55:11.440
Oh my God, even worse.
天哪，更糟糕。

1080
00:55:11.440 --> 00:55:14.970

1081
00:55:14.970 --> 00:55:18.180
All of these lines do nothing for you.
这些条目对你一点帮助都没有。

1082
00:55:18.180 --> 00:55:21.840
They waste an opportunity for you to tell people--
它们浪费了一次让你告诉别人——

1083
00:55:21.840 --> 00:55:23.730
to leave people with what you--
让听众记住你的——

1084
00:55:23.730 --> 00:55:26.360
with who you are.
记住你是谁的机会。

1085
00:55:26.360 --> 00:55:29.000
Well, what about this?
那这张怎么样？

1086
00:55:29.000 --> 00:55:31.450
Is this a good one?
这张算好的吗？

1087
00:55:31.450 --> 00:55:34.660
It might seem so at first, but here's the problem.
乍看之下好像不错，但问题在这里。

1088
00:55:34.660 --> 00:55:38.340

1089
00:55:38.340 --> 00:55:41.040
If you say these are my conclusions,
如果你说“以下是我的结论”，

1090
00:55:41.040 --> 00:55:42.960
these are perfectly legitimate conclusions
这些结论本身完全合情合理，

1091
00:55:42.960 --> 00:55:44.910
that nobody cares about.
但没人在乎。

1092
00:55:44.910 --> 00:55:47.557
What they care about is what you have done.
他们在乎的是你做了什么。

1093
00:55:47.557 --> 00:55:49.140
And that's why your final slide should
所以你的最后一张幻灯片应该

1094
00:55:49.140 --> 00:55:53.460
have this label, contributions.
用“贡献”这个标题。

1095
00:55:53.460 --> 00:55:56.510
It's a mirror of what I said over there about how job talks
这和我之前讲的、关于求职演讲（job talks）

1096
00:55:56.510 --> 00:55:57.830
ought to be like a sandwich.
应该像三明治一样。

1097
00:55:57.830 --> 00:55:59.460
And the final slide, the one that's
而最后一张幻灯片，就是

1098
00:55:59.460 --> 00:56:02.240
up there while people are asking questions and filing out,
人们提问和陆续离开时显示的那张，

1099
00:56:02.240 --> 00:56:05.470
it ought to be the one that has your contributions on it.
应该是写着你的贡献的那张。

1100
00:56:05.470 --> 00:56:09.140
Here's an example from my own stump speech.
这是我自己的竞选演讲中的一个例子。

1101
00:56:09.140 --> 00:56:13.020
Yeah, this is what I talk about a lot.
是的，这是我经常讲的内容。

1102
00:56:13.020 --> 00:56:16.912
Yes, here are the things that I typically demonstrate.
是的，这些是我通常演示的内容。

1103
00:56:16.912 --> 00:56:21.160

1104
00:56:21.160 --> 00:56:23.146
And I wait for people to read it.
然后我等大家读完。

1105
00:56:23.146 --> 00:56:27.616
And the final element there is this is what we get out of it,
最后那个元素是：这是我们从中得到的，

1106
00:56:27.616 --> 00:56:32.780
so that's an example of a contribution slide.
这就是贡献幻灯片的例子。

1107
00:56:32.780 --> 00:56:37.670
All right, now, what about the other part?
好的，那另一部分呢？

1108
00:56:37.670 --> 00:56:39.740
You got your final slide up there.
你已经把最后一张幻灯片放上去了。

1109
00:56:39.740 --> 00:56:41.150
It's a contribution slide.
它是一张贡献幻灯片。

1110
00:56:41.150 --> 00:56:44.430
Somehow you have to tell people you're finished.
你需要用某种方式告诉听众你讲完了。

1111
00:56:44.430 --> 00:56:47.551
So let's see it, check out a few possibilities.
我们来看看几种可能性。

1112
00:56:47.551 --> 00:56:50.140

1113
00:56:50.140 --> 00:56:54.700
One thing you could do in the final words
你可以在最后说的话中做一件事，

1114
00:56:54.700 --> 00:56:56.000
is you could tell a joke.
就是讲个笑话。

1115
00:56:56.000 --> 00:56:59.840

1116
00:56:59.840 --> 00:57:02.232
It's OK.
没问题。

1117
00:57:02.232 --> 00:57:04.565
By the time you're done, people have adjusted themselves
当你讲完时，大家已经调整好自己

1118
00:57:04.565 --> 00:57:05.728
to your voice parameters.
来适应你的声音参数。

1119
00:57:05.728 --> 00:57:06.770
They're ready for a joke.
他们准备好听笑话了。

1120
00:57:06.770 --> 00:57:10.040

1121
00:57:10.040 --> 00:57:13.010
I was sitting in another bar, this time in Austin, Texas
我坐在另一个酒吧里，这次是在德州奥斯汀，

1122
00:57:13.010 --> 00:57:16.310
with a colleague of mine named Doug Lenat.
和我的一位同事道格·勒纳特一起。

1123
00:57:16.310 --> 00:57:18.880
And Doug's a fantastic speaker.
道格是个非常出色的演讲者。

1124
00:57:18.880 --> 00:57:21.920
And so I said to Doug, Doug, you're
于是我对道格说，道格，你

1125
00:57:21.920 --> 00:57:24.860
a fantastic speaker, what's your secret?
是个出色的演讲者，你的秘诀是什么？

1126
00:57:24.860 --> 00:57:29.440
And he said, oh, I always finish with a joke,
他说，哦，我总是以笑话收尾，

1127
00:57:29.440 --> 00:57:32.120
and that way, people think they've had fun the whole time.
这样大家就以为整个过程都很开心。

1128
00:57:32.120 --> 00:57:35.480
[LAUGHTER]
[笑声]

1129
00:57:35.480 --> 00:57:39.540
So yeah, a joke will work down there.
所以，是的，最后讲个笑话会有效。

1130
00:57:39.540 --> 00:57:40.580
How about this one?
这个怎么样？

1131
00:57:40.580 --> 00:57:50.770

1132
00:57:50.770 --> 00:57:51.270
Thank you.
谢谢。

1133
00:57:51.270 --> 00:57:54.030

1134
00:57:54.030 --> 00:57:55.650
I don't recommend it.
我不推荐这样做。

1135
00:57:55.650 --> 00:57:58.410
It's a weak move.
这是较弱的一招。

1136
00:57:58.410 --> 00:58:00.330
You will not go to hell if you conclude
如果你以说

1137
00:58:00.330 --> 00:58:02.730
your talk by saying thank you, but it's a weak move,
谢谢来收尾，你不会下地狱，

1138
00:58:02.730 --> 00:58:04.880
and here's why.
但这是较弱的一招，原因如下。

1139
00:58:04.880 --> 00:58:08.460
When you say thank you, even worse, thank you for listening,
当你说谢谢时，更糟的是，谢谢你们的聆听，

1140
00:58:08.460 --> 00:58:10.350
it suggests that everybody has stayed
这暗示大家一直留着

1141
00:58:10.350 --> 00:58:14.310
that long out of politeness and that they had a profound desire
这么久只是出于礼貌，而他们非常渴望

1142
00:58:14.310 --> 00:58:15.632
to be somewhere else.
去别的地方。

1143
00:58:15.632 --> 00:58:17.340
But they're so polite, they stuck it out.
但他们很有礼貌，硬撑着听完了。

1144
00:58:17.340 --> 00:58:20.430
And that's what you're thanking them for.
而你谢的正是这个。

1145
00:58:20.430 --> 00:58:21.990
So once wild applause has started,
所以一旦热烈的掌声响起，

1146
00:58:21.990 --> 00:58:24.240
you can mouth a thank you, and there's
你可以无声地说声谢谢，这样

1147
00:58:24.240 --> 00:58:25.380
nothing wrong with that.
也没什么不妥。

1148
00:58:25.380 --> 00:58:29.992
But the last thing you do should not be saying thank you.
但你最后做的事不应该是说谢谢。

1149
00:58:29.992 --> 00:58:31.950
Now, you say to me, well, doesn't everybody say
现在你会对我说，难道不是每个人都

1150
00:58:31.950 --> 00:58:33.570
thank you?
说谢谢吗？

1151
00:58:33.570 --> 00:58:37.800
Well, what everybody does is not necessarily the right thing.
嗯，每个人都做的未必是正确的。

1152
00:58:37.800 --> 00:58:40.110
And I like to illustrate how some talks can
我喜欢举例说明，有些演讲可以

1153
00:58:40.110 --> 00:58:41.580
end without saying thank you.
不以说谢谢来收尾。

1154
00:58:41.580 --> 00:58:45.210
I like to draw from political speeches,
我喜欢引用政治演讲的例子，

1155
00:58:45.210 --> 00:58:47.956
but the ones that I've heard recently aren't so good, so--
但我最近听到的那些不太好，所以——

1156
00:58:47.956 --> 00:58:50.240
[LAUGHTER]
[笑声]

1157
00:58:50.240 --> 00:58:55.500
So I'm going to have to go go back a little bit.
所以我得往回找一点。

1158
00:58:55.500 --> 00:58:57.010
So here is Governor Christie.
这位是克里斯蒂州长。

1159
00:58:57.010 --> 00:59:03.680
He gave the Republican keynote address one year.
他曾在某年发表共和党主题演讲。

1160
00:59:03.680 --> 00:59:05.040
This is the end of his talk.
这是他演讲的结尾。

1161
00:59:05.040 --> 00:59:07.214
Let's see what he does.
我们看看他怎么做。

1162
00:59:07.214 --> 00:59:08.966
[APPLAUSE]
[掌声]

1163
00:59:08.966 --> 00:59:11.920
CHRIS CHRISTIE: And together, everybody, together.
克里斯·克里斯蒂：大家团结起来，一起团结。

1164
00:59:11.920 --> 00:59:16.060
We will stand up once again for American greatness
我们将再次为美国的伟大而奋起，

1165
00:59:16.060 --> 00:59:18.190
for our children and grandchildren.
为了我们的孩子和孙辈。

1166
00:59:18.190 --> 00:59:20.583
God bless you, and God bless America.
愿上帝保佑你们，愿上帝保佑美国。

1167
00:59:20.583 --> 00:59:23.241
[APPLAUSE]
[掌声]

1168
00:59:23.241 --> 00:59:27.260
PATRICK WINSTON: So that's a classic benediction ending.
帕特里克·温斯顿：这就是经典的祝祷式结尾。

1169
00:59:27.260 --> 00:59:30.620
God bless you, God bless America.
愿上帝保佑你们，愿上帝保佑美国。

1170
00:59:30.620 --> 00:59:32.610
Now, I don't want to be partisan about this.
现在，我不想在这件事上偏袒任何一方。

1171
00:59:32.610 --> 00:59:35.570
So I think I'd better switch to the keynote address
所以我想还是换成民主党大会的

1172
00:59:35.570 --> 00:59:38.840
in the Democratic Convention.
主题演讲。

1173
00:59:38.840 --> 00:59:42.050
It was delivered that year by Bill Clinton, who knows
那一年的演讲者是比尔·克林顿，他很懂

1174
00:59:42.050 --> 00:59:45.010
something about how to speak.
如何演讲。

1175
00:59:45.010 --> 00:59:46.380
[APPLAUSE]
[掌声]

1176
00:59:46.380 --> 00:59:48.940
BILL CLINTON: If that is what you want,
比尔·克林顿：如果这是你们想要的，

1177
00:59:48.940 --> 00:59:52.300
if that is what you believe, you must vote,
如果这是你们相信的，你们必须投票，

1178
00:59:52.300 --> 00:59:57.046
and you must re-elect President Barack Obama.
你们必须再次选举巴拉克·奥巴马为总统。

1179
00:59:57.046 --> 00:59:58.588
God bless you, and God bless America.
愿上帝保佑你们，愿上帝保佑美国。

1180
00:59:58.588 --> 01:00:00.061
[APPLAUSE]
[掌声]

1181
01:00:00.061 --> 01:00:03.989
[LAUGHTER]
[笑声]

1182
01:00:03.989 --> 01:00:09.390

1183
01:00:09.390 --> 01:00:11.410
PATRICK WINSTON: Now, watch this.
帕特里克·温斯顿：现在，注意看。

1184
01:00:11.410 --> 01:00:13.297
Let's go back a little bit and redo it.
我们往回退一点，再看一遍。

1185
01:00:13.297 --> 01:00:15.130
What I want you to see is that at one point,
我想让你们看到的是，在某个时刻，

1186
01:00:15.130 --> 01:00:18.430
he seems to be almost pressing his lips together, forcing
他似乎几乎要抿紧嘴唇，强迫自己

1187
01:00:18.430 --> 01:00:20.315
himself not to say thank you.
不要说谢谢。

1188
01:00:20.315 --> 01:00:22.690
Then there's another place where he does a little salute.
然后在另一个地方，他做了个小敬礼。

1189
01:00:22.690 --> 01:00:24.784
So watch for those this time around.
所以这次请留意这些地方。

1190
01:00:24.784 --> 01:00:25.650
[APPLAUSE]
[掌声]

1191
01:00:25.650 --> 01:00:28.210
BILL CLINTON: If that is what you want,
比尔·克林顿：如果这是你们想要的，

1192
01:00:28.210 --> 01:00:31.470
if that is what you believe, you must vote,
如果这是你们相信的，你们必须投票，

1193
01:00:31.470 --> 01:00:36.310
and you must re-elect President Barack Obama.
你们必须再次选举巴拉克·奥巴马为总统。

1194
01:00:36.310 --> 01:00:38.470
God bless you., and God bless America.
愿上帝保佑你们，愿上帝保佑美国。

1195
01:00:38.470 --> 01:00:39.625
[APPLAUSE]
[掌声]

1196
01:00:39.625 --> 01:00:42.208
PATRICK WINSTON: That's where he's pursing his lips.
帕特里克·温斯顿：他就是在那儿抿嘴的。

1197
01:00:42.208 --> 01:00:43.186
[LAUGHTER]
[笑声]

1198
01:00:43.186 --> 01:00:45.142
There's the salute.
这就是敬礼。

1199
01:00:45.142 --> 01:00:49.455
[LAUGHTER]
[笑声]

1200
01:00:49.455 --> 01:00:50.830
Yeah, I think that's pretty good.
是的，我觉得挺不错。

1201
01:00:50.830 --> 01:00:52.640
Now, what are we gonna take away from this?
那么，我们能从中学到什么？

1202
01:00:52.640 --> 01:00:57.910
Well, I suppose I could conclude this talk by saying God bless
嗯，我想我可以这样结束演讲：

1203
01:00:57.910 --> 01:01:00.960
you, and God bless the Massachusetts Institute
说"愿上帝保佑你们，保佑麻省理工学院"，

1204
01:01:00.960 --> 01:01:04.732
of Technology, but it might not work so well.
但效果可能不太好。

1205
01:01:04.732 --> 01:01:06.190
But what you can get out of this is
但你能从中学到的是

1206
01:01:06.190 --> 01:01:08.110
you don't have to say thank you.
你不必非要说"谢谢"。

1207
01:01:08.110 --> 01:01:09.850
There are other things you can do.
你还可以做别的事情。

1208
01:01:09.850 --> 01:01:13.060
And it's interesting that over time, people figure this out,
有趣的是，随着时间推移，人们会慢慢明白这一点，

1209
01:01:13.060 --> 01:01:15.730
and there's some stock ways of ending things.
也有了一些固定的收尾方式。

1210
01:01:15.730 --> 01:01:21.180
So in the Catholic church, and the good old Latin mass,
比如在天主教会，还有那古老的拉丁文弥撒中，

1211
01:01:21.180 --> 01:01:27.990
it ended with ite missa est, which
结束时会说"ite missa est"，

1212
01:01:27.990 --> 01:01:31.380
translates approximately to OK, the mass is over,
这句话大致翻译为"好了，弥撒结束了，

1213
01:01:31.380 --> 01:01:32.718
you can go home now.
你们可以回家了"。

1214
01:01:32.718 --> 01:01:34.470
[LAUGHTER]
[笑声]

1215
01:01:34.470 --> 01:01:37.440
And of course, at musical concerts,
当然，在音乐会上，

1216
01:01:37.440 --> 01:01:42.230
you know that it's time to clap not at the end of the song,
你知道该鼓掌的时刻不是歌曲结束，

1217
01:01:42.230 --> 01:01:45.810
but rather when the conductor goes over and shakes hands
而是当指挥走过去跟

1218
01:01:45.810 --> 01:01:47.280
with the concert master.
乐队首席握手的时候。

1219
01:01:47.280 --> 01:01:50.850
Those are conventions that tell you that the event is over.
这些都是告诉你活动已经结束的惯例。

1220
01:01:50.850 --> 01:01:53.470

1221
01:01:53.470 --> 01:01:58.500
So those are all possibilities for here.
所以这些都是这里可以用到的收尾方式。

1222
01:01:58.500 --> 01:02:00.180
But one more possibility, and that
但还有一种方式，那就是

1223
01:02:00.180 --> 01:02:07.320
is that you can salute the audience.
你可以向观众敬礼。

1224
01:02:07.320 --> 01:02:09.070
And by that, I mean, you can say something
所谓敬礼，是指你可以讲一些

1225
01:02:09.070 --> 01:02:13.060
about how much you value your time at a place.
关于你有多珍视在此地时光的话。

1226
01:02:13.060 --> 01:02:20.000
So I could say, well, it's been great fun being here.
比如我可以说，能来到这里非常愉快。

1227
01:02:20.000 --> 01:02:22.780
It's been fascinating to see what you folks are doing here
看你们在这里做的事情很有意思，

1228
01:02:22.780 --> 01:02:24.190
at MIT.
在 MIT 这里。

1229
01:02:24.190 --> 01:02:27.535
I've been much stimulated and provoked
你们提出的问题让我深受启发，

1230
01:02:27.535 --> 01:02:29.410
by the kinds of questions you've been asking,
也让我受到很大触动，

1231
01:02:29.410 --> 01:02:30.800
it's been really great.
这感觉真的很棒。

1232
01:02:30.800 --> 01:02:33.380
And I look forward to coming back
我也期待着将来

1233
01:02:33.380 --> 01:02:35.210
on many occasions in the future.
有机会再来这里。

1234
01:02:35.210 --> 01:02:36.693
So that salutes the audience.
这就是对观众的敬礼。

1235
01:02:36.693 --> 01:02:37.360
You can do that.
你也可以这样做。

1236
01:02:37.360 --> 01:02:40.670

1237
01:02:40.670 --> 01:02:42.520
Well, there it is.
嗯，就是这样。

1238
01:02:42.520 --> 01:02:45.260
You know what?
你们知道吗？

1239
01:02:45.260 --> 01:02:46.940
I'm glad you're here.
我很高兴你们来了。

1240
01:02:46.940 --> 01:02:48.830
And the reason is by being here, I
原因是，你们来到这里，

1241
01:02:48.830 --> 01:02:51.590
think you have demonstrated an understanding
我觉得你们已经表明了一种认识：

1242
01:02:51.590 --> 01:02:54.770
that how you present and how you package your ideas is
那就是如何呈现和包装你的想法

1243
01:02:54.770 --> 01:02:56.510
an important thing.
是一件重要的事。

1244
01:02:56.510 --> 01:02:58.013
And I salute you for that.
为此我要向你们敬礼。

1245
01:02:58.013 --> 01:03:03.500
[LAUGHTER] And I suggest that you come back again and bring
[笑声]我建议你们下次再来时，

1246
01:03:03.500 --> 01:03:05.990
your friends.
也带上你们的朋友。

1247
01:03:05.990 --> 01:03:09.340
[APPLAUSE]
[掌声]

1248
01:03:09.340 --> 01:03:42.000

